1
00:00:00,610 --> 00:00:03,050
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:03,810
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:04,230 --> 00:00:11,730
My name is Catherine and if you've been following along with us recently, you'll know that we have a series about interviewing in English.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,900
And so today we have the very last episode of that series.

5
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:16,090
That's right.

6
00:00:16,900 --> 00:00:25,110
Today is the last part of our interview series and we're basically just saying goodbye and we're doing some other things when you're leaving an interview.

7
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:26,670
It's very important the things that you say.

8
00:00:27,010 --> 00:00:33,210
That's right because this is the last thing that you're going to do in the interview so it's what your interviewer will remember.

9
00:00:33,510 --> 00:00:36,750
So let's take a listen to today's dialogue and we'll be back in a moment.

10
00:00:41,470 --> 00:00:44,690
Well Rebecca, is there anything else you need to know for now?

11
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:46,490
I don't think so Mr. Parsons.

12
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:49,230
I think you have covered all the main points for me.

13
00:00:49,750 --> 00:00:51,010
Okay, well listen.

14
00:00:51,490 --> 00:00:53,490
Here's my business card with my mobile number.

15
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:57,290
If any other questions spring to mind, don't hesitate to contact me.

16
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:00,110
Of course you can also call Ms. Childs too.

17
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:00,770
Great.

18
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:04,100
When can I expect to hear from you?

19
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:11,250
Well we are finishing the short list interviews tomorrow so we will certainly have a decision made by early next week.

20
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:15,360
Ms. Childs will call you to discuss more on Monday or Tuesday.

21
00:01:15,360 --> 00:01:15,830
How does that sound?

22
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:17,270
That sounds perfect.

23
00:01:17,910 --> 00:01:20,710
Thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me Mr. Parsons.

24
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:22,790
The pleasure is all mine Rebecca.

25
00:01:23,530 --> 00:01:24,890
I hope to hear from you very soon.

26
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:25,850
Absolutely.

27
00:01:26,470 --> 00:01:27,370
Thanks for coming Rebecca.

28
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:29,600
Goodbye.

29
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,600
Alright, great.

30
00:01:33,710 --> 00:01:35,070
So really good things from Rebecca.

31
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:38,390
She said some very interesting things, key things there.

32
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:41,410
So why don't we take a look at those on Language Takeaway.

33
00:01:43,190 --> 00:01:44,510
Language Takeaway.

34
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:52,420
At the beginning of the end of the interview, Rebecca says, listen, I think you have covered all the main points for me.

35
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:55,500
So this is after Mr. Parsons asks her if she has questions.

36
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,520
And this word covered is very important.

37
00:01:59,000 --> 00:01:59,380
That's right.

38
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:07,560
So if we talk about cover, to cover the main points or to cover a topic, it means to discuss it thoroughly or to have it finished.

39
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:08,880
Right.

40
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:11,900
So if I still have questions, we have not covered everything.

41
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:17,040
But if I did ask all the questions that I have, then we've covered everything.

42
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,160
So the verb here is to cover.

43
00:02:19,160 --> 00:02:19,380
That's right.

44
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:21,160
Okay.

45
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:25,040
So Rebecca says, well, you know what, here's my business card with my mobile number.

46
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:29,640
So this word business card, key element of any interview or any business situation.

47
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,440
As you say, a key part of any business interaction.

48
00:02:33,040 --> 00:02:42,670
So a business card is a very small card, usually thick, and it has a person's name, job, phone number and sometimes email address now on the card.

49
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:47,960
So the information can change, but it's a contact card basically for one person.

50
00:02:47,960 --> 00:02:48,960
That's right.

51
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:51,890
So it's important to have around, especially in situations like this.

52
00:02:52,710 --> 00:02:52,990
Okay.

53
00:02:53,310 --> 00:03:00,550
And moving on, he also told her that if she has any other questions to not hesitate and to contact him.

54
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,170
This is a very common phrase.

55
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:06,980
Don't hesitate to contact me or don't hesitate to ask.

56
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:07,140
That's right.

57
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:14,600
So to hesitate means to be nervous about something or to maybe not do it because you're uncomfortable.

58
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:22,800
So, you know, Marco, I want to ask you something, but I'm not sure if I want to ask you, maybe you'll be angry or so.

59
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:24,140
I'm hesitating.

60
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:24,640
Right.

61
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:26,440
Okay.

62
00:03:26,440 --> 00:03:27,440
The verb here is to hesitate.

63
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:28,440
You have doubts.

64
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:29,440
I have doubts.

65
00:03:29,440 --> 00:03:31,940
And so he says, don't hesitate to contact me.

66
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:35,470
That means any questions, any issues, please call me.

67
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:36,690
Okay, very good.

68
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:41,290
And Mr. Parson said that they are finishing the short list of interviews tomorrow.

69
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,550
So what does it mean a short list?

70
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:54,140
Well, a short list is something we talk about during the interview process because the interviewer, so in this case, Mr. Parsons will have a long list of people who want the job.

71
00:03:54,140 --> 00:04:01,130
And maybe after the first interview, he will cut the list down to a very small number of people, 10 or 15.

72
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:03,530
And that is called the short list.

73
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:09,990
So those are the people who did well on the first interview, but who maybe should come back for a second or third interview.

74
00:04:10,330 --> 00:04:10,850
That's right.

75
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,680
Okay, very good.

76
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:13,510
A lot of great things there.

77
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,030
Why don't we take a break?

78
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:16,930
Let's go back and listen to the dialogue.

79
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:19,090
Let's see what Mr. Parsons and Rebecca said.

80
00:04:19,650 --> 00:04:24,190
And we'll be back with some really good phrases that you can close off an interview with.

81
00:04:29,560 --> 00:04:32,760
Well, Rebecca, is there anything else you need to know for now?

82
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:35,280
I don't think so, Mr. Parsons.

83
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,300
I think you have covered all the main points for me.

84
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:39,080
Okay, well, listen.

85
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:41,560
Here's my business card with my mobile number.

86
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,360
If any other questions spring to mind, don't hesitate to contact me.

87
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,160
Of course, you can also call Ms. Childs, too.

88
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:50,280
Great.

89
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:52,150
When can I expect to hear from you?

90
00:04:52,790 --> 00:04:59,350
Well, we are finishing the short list interviews tomorrow, so we will certainly have a decision made by early next week.

91
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:03,440
Ms. Childs will call you to discuss more on Monday or Tuesday.

92
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:03,890
How does that sound?

93
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:05,370
That sounds perfect.

94
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:08,770
Thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me, Mr. Parsons.

95
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:10,850
The pleasure is all mine, Rebecca.

96
00:05:11,590 --> 00:05:12,930
I hope to hear from you very soon.

97
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:13,890
Absolutely.

98
00:05:14,530 --> 00:05:15,410
Thanks for coming, Rebecca.

99
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:19,640
Goodbye.

100
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:22,920
All right, we're back.

101
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:25,920
So now let's take a look at those phrases we mentioned on Fluency Builder.

102
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:28,220
Fluency Builder.

103
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:35,200
So let's go back to the beginning where Mr. Parsons is talking about questions that Rebecca might have.

104
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:41,720
And so Mr. Parsons says, if any other questions spring to mind, don't hesitate to contact me.

105
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:43,300
So this phrase, spring to mind, is very interesting.

106
00:05:43,660 --> 00:05:44,020
What does this mean?

107
00:05:44,340 --> 00:05:52,180
Well, basically if something springs to your mind or springs to mind, it means that it comes very suddenly and quickly into your mind.

108
00:05:52,380 --> 00:05:53,460
It just pops into your head.

109
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:54,760
Wow.

110
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:57,660
So to spring or springs in this case is like jumping.

111
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:02,230
So something hits your mind very quickly, comes into your mind very quickly.

112
00:06:02,530 --> 00:06:08,830
So oh, I don't really have any ideas, Marco, for that party next week, but if anything springs to mind, you should let me know.

113
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:10,280
Okay.

114
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:15,790
If you have any sudden ideas or anything you suddenly remember, that's what it means to spring to mind.

115
00:06:16,650 --> 00:06:16,770
Okay.

116
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:29,880
So taking a look at these expressions that Rebecca used when she's leaving, for example, you know that you finished the interview and a typical question that you want to make is, when can I expect to hear from you?

117
00:06:30,380 --> 00:06:36,800
This is a great question at the end of an interview because it's basically a way of saying, listen, I'm interested in this job.

118
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:39,500
When will you call me about it?

119
00:06:39,880 --> 00:06:45,680
And so the phrase, when can I expect to hear from you means, when will you tell me your decision?

120
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:46,680
That's right.

121
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:49,840
But it's a lot more polite and formal and better than saying, when are you going to call me?

122
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:57,580
Yeah, you would never say that in an interview because it makes you sound desperate, like you really need the job.

123
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:00,870
In this case, you know, when can I expect to hear from you?

124
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:04,330
It's like, oh, well, you know, I hope that you'll get in touch with me.

125
00:07:04,450 --> 00:07:04,870
That's right.

126
00:07:05,490 --> 00:07:05,890
Okay.

127
00:07:06,640 --> 00:07:12,080
So she explained that they're shortlisting everything and that they will be in touch with her very soon.

128
00:07:12,080 --> 00:07:13,290
And she said, wow, that sounds perfect.

129
00:07:14,210 --> 00:07:17,550
Thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me.

130
00:07:18,140 --> 00:07:23,200
Okay, so this phrase, thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me, it's very long.

131
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:25,180
But you know what thank you very much means.

132
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,380
The key here is for taking the time to do something.

133
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:29,880
Now what does this mean?

134
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,620
What's another way we can use this?

135
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:36,480
It's basically saying thank you so much for giving me an opportunity to talk to you.

136
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:39,160
I know you're very busy and I know you don't have a lot of time.

137
00:07:39,540 --> 00:07:42,520
So thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me.

138
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:43,100
Right.

139
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:45,240
So you could even use this with a teacher at school.

140
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:49,940
You know, you have a question, your teacher is busy, but your teacher makes some time to meet with you.

141
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:56,120
You say, hey, listen, professor or listen, teacher, thank you so much for taking the time to speak to me.

142
00:07:56,780 --> 00:08:00,640
You're just demonstrating that you appreciate the effort and the time.

143
00:08:01,220 --> 00:08:02,660
And you're being very polite.

144
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:03,760
That's right.

145
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:05,520
Okay.

146
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:14,240
And then the last phrase, when Rebecca is leaving the room, they're shaking hands maybe and Rebecca says, I hope to hear from you very soon.

147
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:15,720
You can say I hope to hear from you soon.

148
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:18,840
But in this case, she's saying, listen, I really want this job.

149
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:22,960
She doesn't want to say, call me, you know, call me tomorrow.

150
00:08:22,960 --> 00:08:24,630
She says, I hope to hear from you soon.

151
00:08:25,010 --> 00:08:27,390
I hope that this works out that I get the job.

152
00:08:27,630 --> 00:08:27,830
Right.

153
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:35,280
So she's basically demonstrating a lot of interest saying, well, I really like the job and I really would like for you to call me and give me good news.

154
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:36,630
So I hope to hear from you soon.

155
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:37,490
Exactly.

156
00:08:37,810 --> 00:08:44,230
So let's listen to today's dialogue one more time and we'll be back in a moment to talk a little bit more about how to end an interview.

157
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:53,920
Well Rebecca, is there anything else you need to know for now?

158
00:08:54,460 --> 00:08:55,740
I don't think so, Mr. Parsons.

159
00:08:56,400 --> 00:08:58,480
I think you have covered all the main points for me.

160
00:08:58,880 --> 00:08:59,320
Okay.

161
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:02,720
Well, listen, here's my business card with my mobile number.

162
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:06,540
If any other questions spring to mind, don't hesitate to contact me.

163
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:09,340
Of course, you can also call Ms. Childs too.

164
00:09:10,120 --> 00:09:11,120
Great.

165
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:13,960
Um, when can I expect to hear from you?

166
00:09:13,970 --> 00:09:20,490
Well, we are finishing the short list interviews tomorrow, so we will certainly have a decision made by early next week.

167
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:24,640
Ms. Childs will call you to discuss more on Monday or Tuesday.

168
00:09:24,640 --> 00:09:25,070
How does that sound?

169
00:09:25,960 --> 00:09:26,490
That sounds perfect.

170
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:29,950
Thank you very much for taking the time to speak to me, Mr. Parsons.

171
00:09:30,640 --> 00:09:32,030
The pleasure is all mine, Rebecca.

172
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:34,110
I hope to hear from you very soon.

173
00:09:34,690 --> 00:09:35,070
Absolutely.

174
00:09:35,710 --> 00:09:36,570
Thanks for coming, Rebecca.

175
00:09:38,880 --> 00:09:42,680
Goodbye.

176
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:44,420
So this is a great way of ending an interview.

177
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:52,200
Rebecca did a really good job, and I think there are a couple of suggestions or tips that you would like to give our users.

178
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:57,480
Yeah, well, one of them is don't, at the end of the interview, ask the person if you have the job.

179
00:09:58,400 --> 00:10:01,740
They'll say, we will be in touch, or thank you so much for coming in.

180
00:10:03,120 --> 00:10:09,120
But if they don't say, we would really like to have you join this company, then you probably shouldn't ask.

181
00:10:09,120 --> 00:10:12,680
You should say, you know, when can I expect to hear from you?

182
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:13,130
That's more polite.

183
00:10:14,240 --> 00:10:19,860
Because I think a lot of times, if you didn't get the job, it makes the interviewer feel very uncomfortable.

184
00:10:19,860 --> 00:10:21,480
But also, it's just a polite thing to do.

185
00:10:21,480 --> 00:10:22,840
So be careful with that.

186
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:29,110
And I've also been told from people in business that if you do receive a job offer, don't accept right away.

187
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:32,610
Say I will think about it, and I'll get back in touch with you.

188
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:33,960
Oh, OK.

189
00:10:33,960 --> 00:10:36,870
Kind of like a strategy maybe to better negotiate the terms and conditions.

190
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:37,590
Exactly.

191
00:10:37,970 --> 00:10:46,990
So you want to take that information, you want to go home, you want to think about it, talk about it with your wife or your husband, and decide, is this something I want?

192
00:10:47,340 --> 00:10:49,400
And then you can contact the employer.

193
00:10:49,650 --> 00:10:49,850
Right.

194
00:10:50,290 --> 00:10:51,790
Yeah, I think that's a very good idea.

195
00:10:52,120 --> 00:11:04,760
And also, I think it's not a wise thing to call or contact the employer asking if you've got the job or asking if they have any news yet, because I think that can be a little bit annoying.

196
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:11,590
But if they say, we will be in touch on Tuesday, and it's a week later, and they never got back to you, send a follow up.

197
00:11:11,800 --> 00:11:11,990
Of course.

198
00:11:12,800 --> 00:11:17,830
A follow up is an email or a letter or even a phone call where you say, listen, I'm really glad I got to meet you today.

199
00:11:18,320 --> 00:11:21,170
I hope that we have the opportunity to work together in the future.

200
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:22,640
Always be in touch.

201
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:23,950
Sincerely, and then your name.

202
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:24,960
Kind of like a reminder.

203
00:11:25,010 --> 00:11:26,770
It's a reminder, like, I want the job.

204
00:11:27,030 --> 00:11:27,870
Here's my information.

205
00:11:28,710 --> 00:11:29,650
Let's do this.

206
00:11:29,790 --> 00:11:32,930
Or at least if I didn't get the job, let me know that I didn't get it.

207
00:11:33,150 --> 00:11:33,730
Yeah, exactly.

208
00:11:34,410 --> 00:11:34,710
All right.

209
00:11:35,320 --> 00:11:36,070
So a lot of great advice here.

210
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:37,110
Great things coming.

211
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:40,990
And we'll be back again with another series.

212
00:11:41,330 --> 00:11:42,990
We're going to have a presentation series.

213
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:45,400
So be sure to keep an eye out for that.

214
00:11:45,400 --> 00:11:49,750
And if you have any other questions or comments, we're always at EnglishPod.com where you can find us.

215
00:11:50,250 --> 00:11:50,810
We'll see you there.

216
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:51,600
Bye-bye.
